---
import StaticPage from '../components/StaticPage.astro';
import { loadData } from '../lib/core.mjs';
import { fmtNum } from '../lib/format.mjs';
import { publishedCity, publishedListing } from '../lib/publish.mjs';
import { SITE } from '../config.mjs';
const { listings, states, cities } = loadData();
const pages = listings.filter(publishedListing);
const towns = cities.filter(publishedCity);
const schema = [{ '@type': 'AboutPage', name: 'About WaterfallsNearYou', url: `${SITE.url}/about`, about: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` }, author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'David F.', url: `${SITE.url}/about` } }];
---
<StaticPage title="About WaterfallsNearYou | Our Data and Mission" h1="About WaterfallsNearYou" eyebrow="Our story" description={`WaterfallsNearYou lists ${fmtNum(listings.length)} named US waterfalls from official USGS data, with full pages for the ${fmtNum(pages.length)} falls that have visitor or written records.`} intro="A free, data-first directory that helps people find waterfalls near them and visit them safely." schema={schema}>
  <h2>Why WaterfallsNearYou exists</h2>
  <p>Search for "waterfalls near me" and you usually get the same handful of famous falls. Meanwhile, thousands of named waterfalls sit a short drive from where people live, recorded on federal topographic maps but missing from most travel sites. WaterfallsNearYou lists every officially named waterfall we can tie to a public record, and it gives a full page to the falls where visitors or the USGS have left something specific to say.</p>

  <h2>What you will find here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>{fmtNum(pages.length)} waterfall pages</strong> for falls with a Google rating from 25 or more reviews, a sourced trip guide, or a substantial USGS description. Each of those pages has coordinates, directions, nearby falls and whatever hours, fees or access notes the sources support.</li>
    <li><strong>{fmtNum(listings.length - pages.length)} more named falls</strong> listed on their state page with county, nearest town and coordinates. They do not get a separate page until there is something specific to add. We would rather leave a field blank than invent a height, a trail length or a quote.</li>
    <li><strong>{fmtNum(towns.length)} town guides</strong> for places with at least three of those full waterfall pages nearby.</li>
    <li><strong>{states.length} state guides</strong> covering the {states.length - 1} states with a named waterfall plus the District of Columbia. Each one opens with how and where the water falls in that state, then the waterfall pages, then the name-and-coordinate list.</li>
    <li><strong>Practical guides</strong> on <a href="/blog/waterfall-safety-tips">safety</a>, <a href="/blog/best-time-to-visit-waterfalls">timing</a>, <a href="/blog/waterfall-photography-tips">photography</a> and <a href="/blog/leave-no-trace-at-waterfalls">Leave No Trace</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where our data comes from</h2>
  <p>Every waterfall location starts with the <strong>U.S. Geological Survey Geographic Names Information System (GNIS)</strong>, the federal government's official repository of domestic geographic names. GNIS records each named waterfall's position, county, topographic quadrangle, alternate historical names and, for some features, written descriptions and naming history. GNIS data is in the public domain.</p>
  <p>Nearby towns come from <strong>GeoNames</strong> (licensed CC BY 4.0), using places with 1,000 or more residents. Distances on this site are straight-line distances; driving and hiking distances are longer.</p>
  <p>Where available, listings are enriched with public business information from Google Maps listings, such as visitor ratings, hours, phone numbers and websites. Those details are refreshed periodically and can change without notice. The photos on waterfall cards are free-licensed waterfall and hiking scenes we host ourselves (Pexels and Unsplash). They are not photographs of that specific waterfall.</p>

  <h2>Who runs WaterfallsNearYou</h2>
  <p>WaterfallsNearYou.com was founded and is run by <strong>David F.</strong> David oversees the site's data, guides and corrections, and every article on the site is published under his name. Questions, corrections and suggestions reach him directly through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</p>

  <h2>Our editorial standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We do not invent details. If a fact such as a height or trail length is not in our sources, we leave it out rather than guess.</li>
    <li>Guides are written to be practical and conservative about safety.</li>
    <li>We clearly separate official USGS records from third-party business details on each page.</li>
    <li>Corrections are welcome. If something is wrong or a waterfall is closed to the public, please <a href="/contact">tell us</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the site is funded</h2>
  <p>WaterfallsNearYou is free to use and supported by advertising served through Google AdSense. Advertisers have no influence over which waterfalls we list or how we describe them. See our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for how advertising cookies work.</p>

  <h2>Start exploring</h2>
  <p>Browse <a href="/states">waterfalls by state</a>, <a href="/search">search by town or name</a>, or head back to the <a href="/">homepage</a> to see waterfalls near your location.</p>
</StaticPage>
